 an application of the Phase-Resolving Model (PRM) in a real situation
The coastal processes around a stone pier are complex and influenced by various factors, such as the pier's shape, the inflow and outflow of water, and the ocean currents. The Phase-Resolving Model (PRM) is a numerical model that simulates these processes by solving the Navier-Stokes equations and the continuity equation. In this case, the PRM is used to simulate the waves and currents around a stone pier in a real situation. The results of this simulation are compared with the measurements taken at the pier, and the accuracy of the model is evaluated. The results show that the PRM can accurately simulate the waves and currents around the pier, and the model is able to reproduce the complex behavior of the water flow and the wave patterns. This is a significant achievement, as it demonstrates the potential of the PRM for real-world applications in coastal engineering and the potential for its use in the design of new piers and coastal structures. Full article activitie Download article: hp_Biologie 2022.pdf Summary file: hp_Biologie 2022_summary_extract.pdf Source code: hp_Biologie 2022_code.pdf

